1 OCCU Mayor of Oaxaca Mexico in 1570, position  as Alcalde Mayor about 1570 (Age 20 years) Oaxaca, Nueva Espana, Alcalde Mayor about 1570 (Age 20 years) Oaxaca, as Alcalde Mayor about 1570 (Age 20 years) Oaxaca
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Pedro de Zamora''' was a former Alcalde Mayor of Oaxaca before coming to reside at Mexico City in the neighborhood of San Sebastián. Among the prominent citizens of Oaxaca in the sixteenth century were members o
2 CONC f the Pérez de Zamora family. Members of this family went by these interchangeable surnames: Zamora, Pérez, Pérez de Zamora, and González.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the latter part of the 1500s there was a Pedro Pérez de Zamora of Oaxaca who was described as being "en corregimiento," which indicates he was somehow involved as a city official. His uncle Luis de Zamora (aka Luis Pérez de Zam
2 CONC ora held various alcaldías mayores for as many as thirty years in the latter half of the 1500s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro served as the Alcalde Mayor of Oaxaca, New Spain, about 1570 before moving to Mexico City in the neighborhood of San Sebastián.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro married Agustina Abarca in Oaxaca, Nueva España. (Agustina Abarca was born about 1544 in Ciudád de México, Nueva España.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Pérez de Zamora was was a son of Alvaro de Zamora (aka Alvaro Pérez de Zamora). Alvaro Pérez de Zamora was a son of Alonso Pérez de Zamora, a conquistador de México. Alvaro's wife was doña Catalina de Ocampo, the third child
2 CONC  of don Sebastián de Saavedra and doña Catalina de Ocampo. (Sebastián de Saavedra was a native of Sevilla, and a son of Sebastián de Saavedra and doña Isabel de Sanabria, native of Carmona —belonging to the Sanabria and Sotomayor 
2 CONC families of this town).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Chávez's New Mexico Families Origins, p. 77, Pedro Zamora served as Mayor of Oaxaca, New Spain, around 1570 before moving to Mexico City in the San Sebastian neighborhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT María de Zamora (ONMF: 77), wife of Bartolomé de Montoya, was identified by Chávez as the daughter of Pedro de Zamora and Agustina de Abarca. Chávez's source indicates that Pedro de Zamora was a former mayor of Oaxaca before comin
2 CONC g to live in Mexico City in the San Sebastián neighborhood. Among the outstanding citizens of Oaxaca in the sixteenth century were members of the Pérez de Zamora family. The members of this family had these interchangeable surname
2 CONC s: Zamora, Pérez, Pérez de Zamora, and González.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of the 16th century there was a certain Pedro Pérez de Zamora de Oaxaca who described himself as "in corregimiento," which indicates that he was somehow involved as a city official. His uncle Luis de Zamora (also known 
2 CONC as Luis Pérez de Zamora) held several senior mayor's offices for up to thirty years in the second half of the 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Pérez de Zamora married in Oaxaca and was the son of Alvaro de Zamora (also known as Alvaro Pérez de Zamora). Álvaro Pérez de Zamora was the son of Alonso Pérez de Zamora, a conqueror of Mexico. Álvaro's wife was Doña Catali
2 CONC na de Ocampo, the third daughter of Don Sebastián de Saavedra and Doña Catalina de Ocampo.
2 CONT (Sebastián de Saavedra was a native of Seville, and son of Sebastián de Saavedra and Doña Isabel de Sanabria, a native of Carmona, belonging to the families Sanabria and Sotomayor).
2 CONT
2 CONT Batltasar Dorantes de Carranza recorded the following information about the Zamora family: "Alonso Pérez de Zamora and Alvaro Pérez, father and son, were among the first conquistadors of New Spain; they left behind many poor sons 
2 CONC and daughters, and they are deprived of towns, which are the peñoles of Oaxaca and others that were given to them in the Province of Guatemala.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorantes de Carranza added this additional information in his account of people from New Spain that may be useful in any additional investigation about the Zamora family: "Alvaro de Zamora left a son and two daughters; the son [Pe
2 CONC dro Perez de Zamoral trapped in Oaxaca; has in the royal treasury, 130 pesos and 200 fanegas of corn, in the town of Tepozotlan. From this house they provide for Pedro Perez de Zamora, in corregimiento, and Luis de Vargas, his bro
2 CONC ther-in-law, in corregimiento. From this house he claims Atanasio de Salcedo, as a brother: all three poor: from this house no one else is provided for." This information indicates that Alvaro de Zamora had a son and two daughters
2 CONC . The son was Pedro Pérez de Zamora, whose brother-in-law was Luis de Vargas. Apparently, Luis de Vargas was married to one of Pedro's sisters, and Atanasio de Salcedo to the second. The three were considered poor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, Dorantes de Carranza gave this information about the father of Álvaro Pérez de Zamora:
2 CONT House of Alonso Pérez de Zamora, neighbor and conqueror of Mexico.
2 CONT Jorge González.............son
2 CONT Luis de Zamora.......son
2 CONT Álvaro de Zamora........son
2 CONT Andrés Pérez Meléndez, for his wife........ grandson
2 CONT Legitimate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beyond the origins, Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Batlasar Doran de Carranza, Summary of the things of the New Spain, Editorial Porrua, S.A., Mexico, 1987, 145, 265 and 374.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro married Agustina Abarca in Oaxaca, New Spain.250 (Agustina Abarca was born around 1544 in Mexico City, New Spain.)
