1 OCCU Maria de Zamora      Born: Abt 1575, San Sebastian, Cuidád de Méjico, Nuevo Espána     Marriage: Bartolomé de Montoya in Tezcoco, Nueva España     Died: After 1609, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España  bullet  Noted events in her life were
2 CONC :  • Dates & Eve
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''María de Zamora''' born in Mexico City at San Sebastian
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria married Bartolome de Montoya, son of Francisco de Montoya and Unknown, in Texcoco, Mexico.  Bartolome de Montoya was born about 1572 in Cantillana, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria came to New Mexico with her family as a member of the second wave of 65 settlers that came to New Mexico in 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT María de Zamora was a Mexican Indian native of the Barrio de San Sebastián in Mexico City, near the acequia of the city. She moved with her parents to Oaxaca when she was seven years old. Later her family relocated to the Pueblo o
2 CONC f Tezcuco, an indigenous community that developed into a multethnic community. This is where she married Bartolomé Montoya, a native of Catillana in the province of Andalucía.
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter of Pedro Zamora, native of Mexico. She brought five children, three boys and two girls, all under sixteen years of age, named Francisco, Diego, Joseph, Lucía, and Petronilla, to New Mexico. She also took, in her service
2 CONC , an Indian girl, Isabel, 10 years old, native of Tecama.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~Parientes, La Herencia, Spring 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Often overlooked is the fact that many of the early families of New Mexico were part Indian and these Indian roots were diverse. Families such as the Montoya, Griego, and Anaya Almazán had roots among the Aztecs of the Valley of M
2 CONC exico. The Luján, López de Gracia, Márquez, Martín Serrano, and Naranjo families were uniquely Nuevomejicano in their blending of Spanish and Pueblo Indian households and extended families.
2 CONT
2 CONT The progenitors of the Montoya family of New Mexico offer one of the earliest examples with specific details about the particular origin of their Indian roots. Bartolomé de Montoya (born 1572) emigrated from Spain, being a native 
2 CONC of Cantillana in the province of Andalucía. His wife, María de Zamora, was a Mexican Indian native of the Barrio de San Sebastián in Mexico City, located near the acequia of the city. The Barrio de San Sebastián was one of four in
2 CONC digenous barrios of Mexico City in the sixteenth century, being formed from the older Aztec barrio of Atzacualco (Tzaqualco) of Tenochtitlán.
2 CONT
2 CONT María de Zamora moved with her parents to Oaxaca when she was seven years of age and then her family relocated to the Pueblo of Tezcuco, an indigenous community that quickly developed into a multi-ethnic community, where she marri
2 CONC ed Montoya. This couple resided in the Barrio de San Lorenzo in the Pueblo de Tezcuco before coming to New Mexico as settlers in 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT In all likelihood, each of the Montoya-Zamora children was born in the Pueblo de Tezcuco. Their daughter, Petronila de Zamora, married Pedro Lucero de Godoy, a man of Spanish background and an encomendero in New Mexico. Diego de M
2 CONC ontoya, the son of Bartolomé de Montoya and María de Zamora, attained the privilege of encomendero of the Pueblo of San Pedro in New Mexico. Encomenderos received tribute from the Pueblo Indians in return for armed military protec
2 CONC tion. Today, most of the people carrying the Montoya and Lucero surnames are descended of Diego and his sister Petronila, respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT _________
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition transcripts. From page 38/40: "In the town of San Gabriel, on the 4th day of the month of January of the year 1607, the said father commissary ordered that María de Zamora, wife of Bartolomé de Montoya, appear before h
2 CONC im so that he might receive her confession, and she was placed under oath as required by law, and she swore and promised to declare the truth, and she was asked the following questions:
2 CONT When asked for her name, age, and home town, she stated that her name is María de Zamora and that she is the wife of Bartolomé de Montoya, that she is 33 years old and a native of Mexico City and was born in the San Sebastián dist
2 CONC rict near the San Sebastián canal.
2 CONT She was asked where she has lived outside Mexico, and who her parents were. She replied that she is the daughter of Pedro de Zamora, a native of Mexico who lived by the said canal in the said district, and her mother’s name is Agu
2 CONC stina Abarca. And she said that the said her father went to be mayor of Oaxaca when this confessor was seven years old, and from there they took her to the village of Tezcuco, where she was raised and married off, and from the sai
2 CONC d village of Tezcuco, where she lived in the San Lorenzo district, her husband brought her to this kingdom."
