1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alonso Baca''' died after 1662 at Rio Abajo, New Mexico, New Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a soldier who came with the second wave of settlers on Christmas Eve of 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT His family was among those settlers who remained loyal to the New Mexico settlement, and stayed in New Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the fourteen conspirators, along with his brother Antonio, who were to face execution on orders of Governor Pacheco in 1643. Alfonso and the 14 others, unlike his brother Antonio, escaped execution. He continued to l
2 CONC ive at his Rio Abajo home as late as 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso married Native American Woman of Analio in 1655 in Bernalillo, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España. (Native American Woman of Analio was born in Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120085969780 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Aug 23 2017, 12:00:06 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso Baca was most likely a younger brother of Antonio Baca. As a young captain he led a small exploratory expedition three hundred leagues into the eastern plains in 1634. He gave his age as 55 in 1644 but he must have been a y
2 CONC ear or two younger. In the mid 1600s he uncovered a serious Indian plot. He was one of 14 conspirators ordered executed by Governor Pacheco in 1643 (Antonio was beheaded). Alonso escaped death. Alonso was still living at his place
2 CONC  in the Rio Abajo district as late as 1662.  
