1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Antonio de Montoya''' born 1647 at Santa Fe, Nuevo Mexico, Nueva España, died 1725 Santa Fe, Nuevo Mexico, Nueva España, age 78
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Lurgarda-Hurtado-De-Salazar/6000000005599019776?through=6000000005599075570 Maria Lurgarda Hurtado De Salazar], daughter of Captain Andrés Hurtado and Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo, about 1638-1
2 CONC 640 in Nuevo México, Nueva España.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Antonio de Montoya and Maria Lurgarda Hurtado De Salazar:
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Andres-Montoya/6000000010341969245?through=6000000003574269566 Andres Montoya]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Juan-de-Montoya/6000000013176884859?through=6000000010341969245 Juan de Montoya]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Antonia-Montoya/6000000001260021481?through=6000000010341969245 Maria Antonia Montoya]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Tomasa-Montoya/6000000003574269566 Tomasa Montoya]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Juana-Montoya/6000000005599075716?through=6000000001278460165 Juana Montoya]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Antonio-de-Montoya/6000000013176821986?through=6000000001278460165 Antonio de Montoya]
2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Nicolasa-de-Montoya/6000000013176977174?through=6000000013176821986 Nicolasa de Montoya]
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio de Montoya was the son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Diego-de-Montoya-I-b-1588/6000000005599075590?through=6000000005599075570 Diego de Montoya] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Ortiz-de-Vera/6000000005599209693?thro
2 CONC ugh=6000000005599075570 Maria Ortiz de Vera]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''Royal Crown Restored'', pg. 37:
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonio de Montoya appeared on the census from Sep to Oct 1680 in La Salineta, Nuevo México, Nueva España.  He was described as between 41 and 43 years old, married, good stature, dark complexion, black eyes, thick beard, somewhat
2 CONC  bald head.  Spanish with spouse, 3 children, 2 servants, family size 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also appeared on the census on 11 Sep 1684 in El Paso, The Pueblo de Nuestra Senora de Guadalupe del Paso, Nuevo México, Nueva España. "The family of Antonio Montoya consists of 6 persons. He has a cornfield which as it appears
2 CONC  will harvest from 6 to 8 fanegas of corn. Clothing, he and his wife very indecent, the other naked."
2 CONT
2 CONT Also appearing on the Don Diego de Vargas census 22 Dec 1692 to 2 Jan 1693 in El Paso del Norte, Nuevo Mexico. Nueva España. First household, three sons, five daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT >First, I (Vargas) visited the household of captain Antonio Montoya, alcalde ordinario de segundo voto, married to María Hurtado, with eight children, three boys and five girls; Juan, eighteen, Andrés, fourteen, and Antonio, nursi
2 CONC ng; Juana, thirteen; María, eleven; Antonia, seven; Nicolasa, five; Tomasa, three.
2 CONT
2 CONT Montoya took part in the reconquest of 1693 as part of Don Diego de Vargas campaign to bring Spain back to New Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''Blood on the Bolders'', pg. 1139:
2 CONT
2 CONT Livestock Distribution: Diego de Vargas, 1 May 1697, Santa Fé, Santa Fé, Nuevo México, Nueva España. ,
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Antonio de Montoya, Maria Hurtado; Andres Montoya, Maria Antonia, Maria, Nicolosa, Tomasa, Antonio, Antonia; 13.5 varas of lana, 11.25 of bayeta, 29 mantas, 25 sheep, 3 cows, 1 bull
2 CONT
2 CONT At some time during the reconquest, in Real of San Lorenzo, he served as Mayordomo of La Conquistador Confraternity, a popular religious society, a.k.a. La Cofradia de Nuestra Senora, del Rosario La Conquistador.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in the military in 1716, during the Moqui Campaign.
2 CONT
2 CONT For his service, he was given a Land Grant, at Abiquiú, Nuevo México, Nueva España, the grant had been given to Antonio Montoya, a captain under Gov. Diego de Vargas, who raised sheep and cattle near the Indian ruins. On May 10, 1
2 CONC 754, the Montoya family lost the Abiquiu property for failure to settle. Governor Cachupin gave the land to the community of Abiquiú for Indians and others to use for grazing of animals and cultivation. To compensate him for the l
2 CONC oss, Antonio Montoya was given land east of the Rio Puerco by Governor Cachupin.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT alcalde ordinario de segundo voto 1693 (Age 46)
2 CONT Alcalde Ordinario along with Alonso Rael de Aguilar
2 CONT January 1, 1707 (Age 60)
