2 CONT #[http://www.geni.com/people/Francisco-Lucero-de-Godoy/6000000000400798519?through=6000000005598882928 Francisco Luis Lucero de Godoy]
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Antonio Lucero de Godoy, I is the son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Pedro-El-Mozo-Lucero-de-Godoy/6000000005598867364?through=6000000008212796914 Pedro Lucero de Godoy] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Petronila-de-Zamo
2 CONC ra/6000000005598888480 Maria Petronila de Zamora]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT He appeared on the Don Diego de Vargas census 22 Dec 1692 to 2 Jan 1693 in El Paso del Norte, Nuevo Méjico. Nueva España.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sixth household, two sons, two daughters, four dependents
2 CONT
2 CONT Sgto. major  '''Juan Lucero de Godoy''', married to doña Isabel Hurtado de Salazar, states that his children are Matias, age thirty; Cayetano Lucero, age four; doña Josefa, fifty; and Barbara, one.  He states that he has four depe
2 CONC ndents: Maria, Juana, Josefa, and Juan.  He states that he is a loyal vassal of his majesty, he is quite ready to enter with his family whenever I, the governor and captain general, enter to settle this kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT To The Royal Crown Restored, pg. 39
2 CONT
2 CONT == Notes ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Kessell, John L. and Hendricks, Rick, editors, To The Crown Restored, The Journals of Don Diego de Vargas, New Mexico, 1692-1694, University of New Mexico Press. 1995, ISBN 0-8263-1559-3
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 1688, Nov. 22 (no.1), El Paso del Norte. Sargento Mayor Juan Lucero de Godoy (63), widow of Da. Juana Carvajal, son of Pedro Lucero de Godoy and Da. Petronila de Zamora, both deceased, and Da. Isabel de Salazar (Salas), single, da
2 CONC ughter of Capt. Andres Hurtado, deceased, and Da. Bernardina de Salas y Trujillo living in El Paso. Both parties related in 3rd degree affinity. The  groom, now Teniente General of this Jurisdiction, had served in New Mexico for m
2 CONC ore than 40 years., and had brought numerous (Indian) souls into the Church; then he had been back in New Mexico in 1687, when more than 200 apostates had returned to the Faith. This to serve as a reason of the dispensation beside
2 CONC s others as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT  He was of noble lineage and "good blood" while the proposed bride lived in dire poverty since her father died. Petition then granted with fine of 100 pesos toward divine cult, the groom promise witnessed by Maese de Campo Felipe 
2 CONC Romero and Alferez Antonio Montoya. Here further testimonies by Felipe Romero (47), Mease de Campo de la Compania Living in El Paso, who says that the groom's first wife, Da. Juana de Carvajal, had died at El Real de S. Lorenzo 5 
2 CONC yrs. before, and that he was the groom's brother-in-law; by Roque de Madrid (44), now Sargento Mayor of the Kingdom (of New Mexico) living in  El Paso, who states that the groom had been married twice before, first to Da. Luisa Ro
2 CONC mero, and then to Da. Juana de Carvajal; also testifying, Antonio Montoya (42) living in El Paso.
