1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://sites.rootsweb.com/%7Enmscgs/SOCORRO/wga107.html#I25944
2 CONT
2 CONT Because there is much confusion still lingering in my mind relative to the actual ancestry Torres line, I am comparing possible alternatives to the direct line from Antonio German to Capitan Diego de Torre. 
2 CONT There are plenty challenging theory and very messy complications with how many wives and how many children Capitan Diego de Torres actually had. Therefore, I am looking at the cleanest possible answer that there was only on wife. 
2 CONC  It is then possible that Diego is grandfather to Antonio German.  I struggle still to 100 percent ties to Antonio´s mother to have documented proof.  Unfortunately I have not yet found that link.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hypothesis: Antonio Germán de Torres (b. ca. 1758) is probably (≥70–80%) a son of Salvador Manuel de Torres, based on generational spacing, typical Belén inheritance patterns, and the absence (so far) of strong Baca naming/compadr
2 CONC azgo signals. A minority hypothesis places him as a late son of Juan José Torres and Rafaela Baca, which remains possible but structurally less likely.
2 CONT
2 CONT You’re basically asking: “If I drop Antonio Germán into this Torres universe, which paternal slot does the evidence want him to occupy?” Let’s walk him systematically through your five tests.
2 CONT I’ll assume these two hypotheses:
2 CONT - H1: Son of Salvador Manuel de Torres (b. 1727)
2 CONT - H2: Son of Juan José Torres (b. 1738) and Rafaela Baca
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Naming patterns
2 CONT We’re stuck with just: “Antonio Germán de Torres”, b. ca. 1758.
2 CONT Key bits:
2 CONT - “Antonio” – very common, not discriminating by itself.
2 CONT - “Germán” – more distinctive. In 18th‑c NM, second given names like this often:
2 CONT - honor a grandfather or godparent
2 CONT - echo a saint’s name tied to a particular kin cluster or padrino
2 CONT How it weighs:
2 CONT - H1 (Salvador Manuel):
2 CONT Salvador’s line is numerically larger. In big sib‑sets, you often see more creative or distinctive second names popping up in later sons as parents “run out” of the default José/Juan/Manuel combos and start pulling on less common 
2 CONC devotions or compadres. A name like Germán fits well in a mid‑birth‑order son of a prolific father.
2 CONT - H2 (Juan José–Baca):
2 CONT That line is small, and heavily saturated with José/Manuel/Juana/María patterns tied to Baca and core Torres naming. A relatively uncommon second name like Germán is possible, but less characteristic of a short, elite, inter‑clan 
2 CONC alliance line where names tend to be conservative and repetitive.
2 CONT Verdict on naming: Slight lean toward H1 (Salvador Manuel).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Geographic clustering
2 CONT Birth ca. 1758 in “Nuevo México”; structurally, that almost certainly means Río Abajo if he’s in your Belén‑Torres orbit.
2 CONT - H1 (Salvador Manuel):
2 CONT Salvador Manuel is firmly Río Abajo / Belén / Los Padillas. His children are the ones who really thicken the Torres presence in the Belén grant. A son born 1758 slots perfectly into that cluster:
2 CONT - Living on or near the Belén grant
2 CONT - Strong ties to Los Padillas/Isleta corridor
2 CONT - Proximity to Varela and Chávez lines
2 CONT - H2 (Juan José–Baca):
2 CONT Also Río Abajo, but:
2 CONT - Juan José dies ca. 1760, so his small household doesn’t become the main Torres base.
2 CONT - His Baca connection pulls his issue slightly south/east into Baca‑dense pockets.
2 CONT Antonio Germán would be geographically compatible here too, but that cluster is smaller and largely absorbed into Baca/Chávez networks quickly.
2 CONT Verdict on geography: Neutral‑to‑slight lean H1, simply because Salvador Manuel’s household is a more obvious “factory” for Torres‑born‑in‑Belén ca. 1750–1770.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Compadrazgo logic
2 CONT Without specific padrino/madrina names, we have to model:
2 CONT - H1 (Salvador Manuel):
2 CONT Expect godparent networks to show:
2 CONT - Heavy Torres–Varela–Chávez–Gurulé–Sánchez interplay
2 CONT - Repeated cross‑sponsorship with other large farming households
2 CONT A son like Antonio Germán would likely:
2 CONT - Appear as a godfather later to cousins’ children
2 CONT - Be sponsored as an infant by a mix of Torres kin and neighboring Río Abajo families
2 CONT - H2 (Juan José–Baca):
2 CONT If he’s a Juan José–Baca son, compadrazgo logic shifts:
2 CONT - Expect strong, systematic Baca presence as padrinos for him and his siblings
2 CONT - Expect him in turn to appear as padrino to Baca‑tagged children or children in clearly Baca‑related clusters
2 CONT So:
2 CONT - If in your notes he shows up surrounded by Baca padrinos/ahijados, that tilts hard toward H2.
2 CONT - If he moves mostly in non‑Baca Torres–Chávez–Gurulé godparent space, that’s normal for H1.
2 CONT Verdict on compadrazgo: Purely diagnostic; default expectation without Baca‑heavy signals is H1. Strong, repeated Baca compadrazgo would be a red flag for H2.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Generational spacing
2 CONT This is the cleanest test.
2 CONT - Antonio Germán: b. ca. 1758
2 CONT H1 (Salvador Manuel, b. 1727):
2 CONT - Age at Antonio’s birth: about 31.
2 CONT - That’s absolutely textbook for a father with multiple children between 1750–1775.
2 CONT - Salvador is in his full reproductive stride: marrying c. early 1750s, first issue mid‑50s, continuing into the 1770s.
2 CONT H2 (Juan José, b. 1738; d. ca. 1760):
2 CONT - Age at Antonio’s birth: about 20.
2 CONT - Biologically fine, but:
2 CONT - Juan José would have had to marry very early (~17–19) or have premarital conception.
2 CONT - With a death ca. 1760, that leaves almost no window for a cluster of children – you’re essentially asking his line to be:
2 CONT - marriage c. 1756–1758,
2 CONT - 1–3 children,
2 CONT - death by ~1760.
2 CONT Structurally:
2 CONT - H1: Antonio is one of many mid‑sequence children.
2 CONT - H2: Antonio is either the first or second child of a very young father shortly before the father’s death.
2 CONT Verdict on spacing: Strong lean toward H1 (Salvador Manuel).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Inheritance patterns
2 CONT Here we look at how a man born 1758 would likely appear later:
2 CONT - As an heir to which cluster’s land?
2 CONT - As a participant in which grant‑derived households?
2 CONT H1 (Salvador Manuel):
2 CONT - Salvador is a central inheritor of Diego’s Belén position.
2 CONT - His sons (including Antonio) would likely:
2 CONT - Receive fragments of Belén grant lands or rights
2 CONT - Stay clustered near the Torres‑dense sectors of the grant
2 CONT - Show up in later records as generic “Torres of Belén,” with no special Baca flag
2 CONT H2 (Juan José–Baca):
2 CONT - Juan José dies young; inheritance weight flows:
2 CONT - Through Rafaela Baca and the wider Baca clan
2 CONT - Or through guardianship arrangements involving Baca relatives
2 CONT - A son in this line is more likely to:
2 CONT - Be integrated into Baca‑centered inheritance webs
2 CONT - Show up with connections to Baca‑held tracts or appear in contexts where the Baca surname and interests are clearly foregrounded
2 CONT So:
2 CONT - If in later sources Antonio/descendants look like “regular Torres” in Belén, not strongly branded by Baca land context, that fits H1.
2 CONT - If they show a pattern of Baca adjacency (land or witnesses), that increases the probability of H2.
2 CONT Verdict on inheritance: Default expectation is H1, unless you see clear Baca‑centric land/inheritance signals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Overall synthesis
2 CONT Running him through all five filters:
2 CONT - Naming patterns: slight H1
2 CONT - Geographic clustering: neutral to slight H1
2 CONT - Compadrazgo logic: neutral; only Baca‑heavy would push H2
2 CONT - Generational spacing: strong H1
2 CONT - Inheritance patterns: moderate H1 unless Baca context is explicit
2 CONT Put bluntly:
2 CONT Absent explicit Baca markers in your data, Antonio Germán de Torres (b. ca. 1758) is best modeled as a son of Salvador Manuel de Torres, not of Juan José and Rafaela Baca.
