2 NICK Francisco Xavier Duran y Chavez
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 APR 1769
2 ADDR
3 STAE Provincia de Nuevo México
3 CTRY Reino de Nueva España
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1838
2 ADDR
3 CITY Los Padillas
3 STAE Nuevo México
3 CTRY Mexico
1 OCCU 2nd Mexican Governor of New Mexico, First Governor of the Departmento de Nuevo México under the independent Republic of Mexico in 1823
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} First Governor of the Departmento de Nuevo México under the independent Republic of Mexico in 1823.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francisco_Xavier_Ch%C3%A1vez
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisco Xavier Chávez was the second géfe político (equivalent to governor) of the territory of Santa Fe de Nuevo México after Mexico gained its independence from Spain in 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisco Xavier Chávez belonged to an old Spanish family that had been prominent in New Mexico since it was created as a province in 1598. He had large holdings of land and livestock and excellent political connections that he le
2 CONC veraged to become one of the dominant traders in the new republic. He had four sons and five daughters. His sons Mariano, José and Antonio José continued and expanded the family business of carrying freight between Mexico and the 
2 CONC United States along the Santa Fe Trail.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1822 the Governor under the Spanish regime, Facundo Melgares, lost the title of governor but was called political and military chief until he retired in June, reporting to the commander at Chihuahua on military matters.
2 CONC  Melgáres called for the election of electors, and on January 28 the electors chose Francisco Perez Serrano y Aguirre as New Mexico's first deputy to the Congress of Mexico. The next day the electors chose the New Mexican legislat
2 CONC ive body, with Francisco Xavier Chavez heading the list. When the legislature met for the first time on 14 April 1822, Chavez served as the first presiding officer. Melgáres left office on 5 July 1822, and Francisco Xavier Chavez 
2 CONC took his place, holding office for just five months, when he was succeeded by Colonel José Antonio Vizcarra.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francisco_Xavier_Ch%C3%A1vez
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisco Xavier Chávez was the second géfe político (equivalent to governor) of the territory of Santa Fe de Nuevo México after Mexico gained its independence from Spain in 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisco Xavier Chávez belonged to an old Spanish family that had been prominent in New Mexico since it was created as a province in 1598. He had large holdings of land and livestock and excellent political connections that he le
2 CONC veraged to become one of the dominant traders in the new republic. He had four sons and five daughters. His sons Mariano, José and Antonio José continued and expanded the family business of carrying freight between Mexico and the 
2 CONC United States along the Santa Fe Trail.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1822 the Governor under the Spanish regime, Facundo Melgares, lost the title of governor but was called political and military chief until he retired in June, reporting to the commander at Chihuahua on military matters.
2 CONC [2] Melgáres called for the election of electors, and on January 28 the electors chose Francisco Perez Serrano y Aguirre as New Mexico's first deputy to the Congress of Mexico. The next day the electors chose the New Mexican legis
2 CONC lative body, with Francisco Xavier Chavez heading the list.[3] When the legislature met for the first time on 14 April 1822, Chavez served as the first presiding officer.[4] Melgáres left office on 5 July 1822, and Francisco Xavie
2 CONC r Chavez took his place, holding office for just five months, when he was succeeded by Colonel José Antonio Vizcarra.
2 CONT
