1 CONT Source: Batlasar Dorantes de Carranza, Sumária relación de la cosas de la Nueva España, Editorial Porrua, S.A., México, 1987, 145, 265 & 374.
0 @N6000000005024494394@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Cpt. Cristóbal Baca, native of Mexico City, Nueva España, and his wife, dona Ana Ortiz y Pacheco, also native of Mexico City, Nueva España, brought 3 daughters and a son with them to New Mexico. Another son, Alonso Baca, was born 
1 CONC in New Mexico. Through him the Baca family name was passed on to following generations.
1 CONT Cristóbal Baca (Vaca) was one of the captains who came to reinforce the Onate colony in 1600. He was the son of Juan de Vaca, born in México City, of good stature, dark complexion, well-featured, and thirty-three years of age. He 
1 CONC brought his wife, three grown daughers, and a small son. His wife was Doña Ana Ortíz, daughter of Francisco Pacheco, also born in México City. 
1 CONT The children were Juana de Zamora, Isabel [de Bohorquez], María de Villanueva, and the boy Antonio, all of them born in México City. With them came a female servant, Ana Verdugo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baca's family was among the few who remained at San Gabriel when the rest of the colonists deserted; Cristóbal himself was very critical of some friars who lead the desertion.  In 1603, he commanded the escort which brought four n
1 CONC ew Franciscans from México City. In 1613, he was acting sindic for the friars. After this, he disappeared from the records, but his descendants begin to fill the annals of New Mexico.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of the three daughers, Juana became the wife of Simon Pérez de Bustillo, Isabel married Don Pedro Duràn y Cháves, and María was the wife of Simon de Abendaño. The family name was passed on down by Antonio and a younger brother, Al
1 CONC onso, born in New Mexico.
1 CONT
1 CONT Captain, legitimate son of Juan de Vaca (possibly the same Juan de Vaca as the one on the Coronado Expedition) , native of the city of México, of good stature and features, swarthy, 33 years old.,
0 @N6000000005024488206@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024494433@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024494424@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Alonso Baca,  a contemporary of Antonio, was to all appearances Antonio's younger brother. As a young Captain, he lead a small exploritory expedition three hundred leagues into the eastern plains in 1634. He gave his age as 55 in 
1 CONC 1644, but must have been a year or two younger. In the middle of the century, he uncovered a serious Indian plot. He was one of fourteen conspirators ordered executed by Governor Pacheco in 1643 (when Antonio died), but all these 
1 CONC fourteen escaped death; Alonso was still living at his place in Rio Abajo district as late as 1662. Nothing more is known about him, not even his wife's name. Cristóbal Baca, mentioned in 1663 as sobrino carnarl of Antonio Baca, w
1 CONC as apparently his son. 
1 CONT ______________________________________
1 CONT When the pueblos rebelled in 1680, there were only four male adult male Bacas listed: Cristóbal, Igacio, Manuel, and José.
0 @N6000000005024488751@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Antonio Ortíz Baca, was a Capitán by 1628 and was twenty-six years old, he said, married and living in Santa Fé. He was the main ring-leader in the anti-Rosas faction which caused this Governor's death, January 25, 1642. He was al
1 CONC so the leader of the people who defied the Governor by barricading themselves with the friars at Santa Domingo pueblo. His turbulent career ended on July 21 1643 when he was beheaded with others in Santa Fé. 
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife was Yumar Pérez de Bustillo, forty years old in 1631. She, too, had come to New Mexico as a child with her parents. Of their three known daughers, Gertrudis married Antonio Jorge, Ana was the wife of Franciso López de Ara
1 CONC gon, and Gregoria married Antonio de Alvizu. What later Bacas were their sons, if any, cannot as yet be ascertained.
0 @N6000000005024494371@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024488473@ NOTE Catholic
0 @N6000000005024494497@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Among the group of four masked men who accompanied Nicolas Ortiz when he murdered Governor Rosas.
1 CONT * PROPERTY: an estancia four leagues (about 12 miles) north of the Isleta Pueblo on the Rio del Norte
1 CONT 1667 [320] [321] 
1 CONT * BIRTH: 1610, Santa Fé, Nuevo México [317] 
1 CONT * DEATH: AFT 1681, Atrisco, Nuevo México [318] [319] 
1 CONT Member of the: 1642 [322] [323] [324] 
1 CONT * EVENT: With the refugees fleeing from the Rio Abajo
1 CONT passed muster: 1680 [325] [326] 
